<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Diwali</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Dipavali, Deepavali, Dīpāvalī</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Diwali, or Dipavali, is the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> festival of lights. It is celebrated over five or six days between around mid-October and mid-November and is associated with the triumph of light over darkness and good over evil; Hindus connect it with the day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama">Rama</a> returned to his kingdom in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayodhya">Ayodhya</a> after defeating the demon king Ravana, and with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lakshmi">Lakshmi</a>, the goddess of prosperity. Jains mark the final liberation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahavira">Mahavira</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikhs</a> the release of Guru Hargobind from a Mughal prison. The festival originated in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-subcontinent">Indian subcontinent</a>, likely as a combination of harvest festivals of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Celebrants light their homes and temples with diyas, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oil-lamp">oil lamps</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/diwali.jpg" alt="Diwali" width="330" height="260" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Subharnab Majumdar · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Rangoli_of_Lights.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The name comes from Sanskrit Dipavali, a compound of dipa, 'lamp, light', and avali, 'a row, series', so 'a row of lights'. The Padma Purana and the Skanda Purana, both composed between the 7th and 10th centuries, mention the festival. In the 7th-century play Nagananda the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harsha">Harsha</a> called it Dipapratipadotsava, when lamps were lit and newly engaged couples received gifts, and in the 9th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajashekhara">Rajasekhara</a>'s Kavyamimamsa described whitewashing and decorating houses, streets and markets with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oil-lamp">oil lamps</a> at night. Inscriptions across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> also name it, among them a 10th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> plate of the Rashtrakuta king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna-iii">Krishna III</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The five-day festival falls between the second half of October and the first half of November, around the new moon of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> lunisolar calendar. It begins on Dhanteras, a day for cleaning homes, lighting lamps near images of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lakshmi">Lakshmi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganesha">Ganesha</a>, and buying utensils and gold. The second day, Naraka Chaturdashi, recalls the killing of the demon Narakasura by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a>. The third day, Lakshmi Pujan, is the darkest night and the height of the festival; Hindus typically feast rather than fast. The fourth day goes by names such as Annakut and Govardhan Puja, and the fifth, Bhai Duj, celebrates the bond of brother and sister.</p><p>Meanings differ by region and faith. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a> connect it with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama">Rama</a>'s return to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayodhya">Ayodhya</a> after fourteen years in exile, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lakshmi">Lakshmi</a>, and in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangladesh">Bangladesh</a> with Kali. Jain tradition places <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahavira">Mahavira</a>'s nirvana in 527 BCE, when eighteen kings gathered for his last teachings ordered lamps lit. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikhs</a> keep Bandi Chhor Divas for the release of Guru Hargobind, and Newar Buddhists worship Lakshmi.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Celebrants clean and decorate their homes, light diyas, make rangoli designs, wear their best clothes, set off fireworks and share mithai. Villages hold melas, or fairs, where artisans and producers trade. The main day is an official holiday in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fiji">Fiji</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guyana">Guyana</a>, Malaysia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritius">Mauritius</a>, Myanmar, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suriname">Suriname</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinidad-and-tobago">Trinidad and Tobago</a>. Diwali is a major cultural event for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> and Jain diaspora. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> has inscribed it on its List of Intangible Cultural Heritage.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Diwali” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Pilgrimage to sacred rivers and cities such as Varanasi on the Ganges, and festivals such as <b>Diwali</b>, the festival of lights, and Holi, the festival of colours, mark the year.</blockquote><a href="/religion/hinduism">Hinduism · Beliefs &amp; Practice</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1757 Ahmad Shah Durrani desecrated the temple, and in 1762 he had it blown up with gunpowder; Sikhs celebrated <b>Diwali</b> in its ruins.</blockquote><a href="/work/golden-temple">Golden Temple · Survival &amp; Rediscovery</a></li><li><blockquote>Hindus connect <b>Diwali</b>, the festival of lights, with Rama's return to Ayodhya, and dramatic enactments called Ramlila take place across India and the Indian diaspora.</blockquote><a href="/work/ramayana">Ramayana · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
